All organisms inherit their genetic makeup through reproduction—parental transfer of DNA fragments between gametes during sexual reproduction, or maternal transmission via egg cells during ovulation before fertilization occurs in mammals such as cows.
Genetic variation results both naturally within populations due to mutations and sexually among members of a species because they receive varying amounts of specific combinations of parental chromosomes. When reproductive events occur randomly throughout generations without regard to fitness consequences, natural selection cannot act upon those variations until certain circumstances arise.
In contrast, artificial selections take place under human control where particular traits become more common than other similar but less desired ones. Artificial selections do not involve random chance;

